\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b SNDDropATCSamples()
\b0  and 
\b SNDInsertATCSamples()
\b0  provide elementary support for synchronizing sound playback to other events.  By skipping or repeating selected samples of an ATC sound while it's playing, you can slightly shrink or stretch its duration, as necessary.  This mechanism is intended for subtle timing adjustments, not for effects like fast forward.  Although dropping or inserting samples shouldn't cause any clicks in the  sound, it will cause some distortion.  A sound in the ATC (Audio Transform Compression) format must be playing for these functions to have any effect.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 If 
\i bySamples
\i0  is zero, one sample is chosen randomly out of the next 256 consecutive samples and omitted (in the case of 
\b SNDDropATCSamples()
\b0 ) or repeated (in the case of 
\b SNDInsertATCSamples()
\b0 ).  This process repeats until 
\i numSamples
\i0  samples have been dropped or inserted.  If 
\i bySamples
\i0  is greater than zero, an attempt is made to drop or add 
\i numSamples
\i0  samples before 
\i bySamples
\i0  more samples of the original sound have played.  Too large a ratio of 
\i numSamples
\i0  to 
\i bySamples
\i0  may produce unwanted results.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 To determine when to drop or insert samples, call 
\b SNDSamplesProcessed()
\b0  to obtain an estimate of how many samples have been played thus far.  Divide this number by the sampling rate to obtain the elapsed time in seconds, and compare the result to the notion of time with which you want to synchronize.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\b\fs28\fi-1540\li2380\fc0\cf0 SEE ALSO	SNDSamplesProcessed(), SNDCompressSound(), SNDSetATCGain(), SNDGetNumberOfATCBands()\

\fs20 \

