\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b SNDStartPlaying()
\b0  initiates the playback of 
\i sound.
\i0   The function returns immediately while the playback continues in a background thread.  During playback, the sound is played on the internal speaker and sent to the stereo line-out jacks.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The 
\i tag
\i0  argument is an arbitrary positive integer that the caller supplies to identify the€playback session in subsequent calls to 
\b SNDWait()
\b0 , 
\b SNDStop()
\b0 , 
\b SNDSamplesProcessed()
\b0 , and 
\b SNDModifyPriority()
\b0 .  You should never set a sound's tag€to 0.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 The value of 
\i priority
\i0  establishes the sound's right to use the playback resources.  The lowest priority is 0, larger numbers signify higher priorities.  Negative priorities are reserved.  A call to 
\b SNDStartPlaying()
\b0  will interrupt a currently playing sound if the new€sound has a higher priority.  If the new sound has a lower priority, the old sound continues and the new sound is put in a sound playback queue.  Sounds in the queue are sorted by priority.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 A nonzero 
\i preempt
\i0  flag is used for urgent sounds, such as system beeps.  Preemption allows a new sound to interrupt a sound that has the same (or lower) priority.  However, if the new sound can't interrupt, it isn't put in the queue.\

\fs20 \

\i\fs28 beginFun
\i0  and 
\i endFun
\i0  are user-defined notification functions that are automatically called when the sound begins playing and when it ends, respectively.  A notification function is defined as an integer function with three arguments:\

\fs20 \

\fs28 	typedef int (*SNDNotificationFun)(SNDSoundStruct *
\i sound
\i0 , int 
\i tag
\i0 , int 
\i err
\i0 ); \

\fs20 \

\fs28 The 
\i sound
\i0  and 
\i tag
\i0  arguments are taken directly from the 
\b SNDStartPlaying()
\b0  call.  The 
\i err
\i0  argument is one of the error codes listed in 
\b SNDSoundError()
\b0  and is generated automatically to inform the notification function of the state of the playback.  The return value is ignored.  The value SND_NULL_FUN should be used to specify no function as either 
\i beginFun
\i0  or 
\i endFun
\i0 .\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 SNDVerifyPlayable()
\b0  returns SND_ERR_NONE if 
\i sound
\i0  can be played without first being converted to another format, sampling rate, or number of channels.  Otherwise, it returns SND_ERR_CANNOT_PLAY.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 The arguments to 
\b SNDStartRecording()
\b0  are like those to 
\b SNDStartPlaying()
\b0 .  The sound resource used for recording is implied by information in 
\i sound
\i0 's header; currently, two configurations are allowed:\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	If the sound is one channel of mulaw format (SND_FORMAT_MULAW) at the CODEC sampling rate (SND_RATE_CODEC), then the recording is made from the CODEC input (the microphone jack at the back of the monitor).\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	If the format is one of the DSP data or compressed formats, the recording is made from the DSP port.  In the case of a compressed format, the sound is compressed according to options set by 
\b SNDSetCompressionOptions()
\b0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 Like playback, recording is performed in a background thread.  The recording completes when the storage allocated for the sound is filled with data.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 For both playback and recording, reserving the appropriate sound device first (through 
\b SNDReserve()
\b0 ) may help eliminate some overhead.  This is particularly true if you're playing a series of sounds.\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 SNDStartRecordingFile()
\b0  is similar to 
\b SNDStartRecording()
\b0 , but the sound is written directly to the file 
\i fileName
\i0 .  The 
\i sound
\i0  argument is used for its size and format information.\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 SNDStop()
\b0  terminates the playback or recording session that has a tag of 
\i tag
\i0 .\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 SNDWait()
\b0  returns only when the playback or recording with a tag of 
\i tag
\i0  has completed.  If 
\i tag
\i0  is 0, all sounds in the sound queue are awaited.  Note that if you call this function from the main thread of an application that has an asynchronous event-driven user interface, the interface will be effectively frozen until this function returns.\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 SNDSamplesProcessed()
\b0  returns the number of samples that have been played or recorded so far in the playback or recording that has the given tag
\i .
\i0  If the tagged sound isn't currently active, 
\f1 -
\f0 1 is returned. \

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 SNDModifyPriority()
\b0  resets the priority, as 
\i newPriority
\i0 , of the playback or recording that has a tag of 
\i tag
\i0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	If no error occurs, SND_ERR_NONE is returned.  Otherwise, an error code, as described in 
\b SNDSoundError()
\b0 , is returned.\

