\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	These functions read the data pointed to by 
\i src
\i0 , swap the order of the bytes that comprise each sample (if necessary) and write the swapped data to 
\i dest
\i0  (
\i src
\i0  and 
\i dest
\i0  can be the same pointer).  The other arguments, taken cumulatively, describe the size of the sound buffer:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\fi-380\li2760\fc0\cf0 ·	
\i sampleCount
\i0  is the number of sample frames, or channel-independent samples.\
·	
\i channelCount
\i0  is the number of channels of sound data.  \
·	
\i dataFormat
\i0  is a format-encoding constant, such as SND_FORMAT_LINEAR_16.\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 The functions return an error code, where SND_ERR_NONE indicates success.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 The ªSoundº and ªHostº terms in these names have these meanings:\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	To play a sound, the sound data must be in ªSoundº format.\
·	To examine sound data, it must be in ªHostº format. \

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 When you read a soundfile or record a sound, the sound data is guaranteed to be in ªSoundº format; if all you want to do is play the soundfile or write the recorded sound to a fileÐin other words, if you don't want to examine the dataÐthen you don't need to call these functions.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 However, if, for example, you read a soundfile in order to look at the value at each sample, you must first call 
\b SNDSwapSoundToHost()
\b0  to convert the sound data to ªHostº format.  If you modify samples in a converted sound, you must re-convert to ªSoundº format, through
\b  SNDSwapHostToSound()
\b0 , before the sound can be played or written to a file.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 Note that you don't have to convert entire soundfiles (or SNDSoundStruct data buffers) at a time.  The arguments let you convert an arbitrary range of samples starting at any sample in a sound data buffer.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 These functions are built upon the general 
\b NXSwap...
\b0  functions that are described in 
\b /NextLibrary/Documentation/NextDev/Concepts/PortabilityGuide
\b0 .  The primary difference between the sound swapping functions and the general swapping functions is that the sound functions let you convert buffers of data, whereas a single call to a general function can convert only one datum.   If you wish, you can call the general functions (iteratively) rather than the sound functions.  The correspondences between the  functions are:\

\fs20 \

\fs4 \

\pard\tx6420\tx10440\f2\b\fs28\li2760\fc0\cf0 Sound function	Analogous general function\

\fs6 \

\f0\b0\fs28 SNDSwapSoundToHost()	NXSwapBigShortToHost()\

\fs20 \

\fs28 SNDSwapHostToSound()	NXSwapHostShortToBig()\

\fs20 \

